{"author":{"id":"f4f7e123ea38957901744390924e1710825e6d55b5113472173231f66fb2d926"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1777790224,"nonce":"ZTFxbE3M+Jwb/r/wTs22gMd/lYU=","title":"Outreach: comp.lang.basic.* USENET + retro forum announcements","message":"Niche but perfectly-aligned audience: long-time BASIC programmers, retro-computing hobbyists, BASIC educators. Different angle and venues from the HN/Lobsters writeup.\n\nUSENET groups (still active enough to matter):\n- comp.lang.basic.misc — general BASIC discussion\n- alt.lang.basic — broader BASIC community\n- comp.os.msdos.programmer — DOS-toolchain context\n- comp.os.freebsd.misc + comp.os.linux.misc — note Linux/BSD support\n\nSDF provides NNTP from the ARPA tier (no MetaARPA needed) — user can post directly via news.sdf.org with tin/slrn/Pan.\n\nRetro forums (web, complementary to USENET):\n- vogons.org/viewforum.php?f=8 (PC software dev)\n- atariage.com/forums (8-bit BASIC nostalgia overlap)\n- 99er.net (TI BASIC crowd, but receptive to GW-BASIC)\n- retrobattlestations subreddit\n- The 8-Bit Guy forum / discord (huge audience for accessible-language revivals)\n\nRequired content (different from HN/Lobsters):\n- Lead with GW-BASIC compatibility, not novelty: 'Programs from your 1985 BASIC magazine listings should run unmodified.'\n- Highlight the AOT compiler — many BASIC dialects are interpreter-only, this isn't\n- DOSBox-X test methodology — earns trust with users who've been burned by 'compatible' tools that aren't\n- Open invitation: 'send me your favorite vintage BASIC program; if it doesn't run, I want a bug report'\n\nGating: HN/Lobsters writeup should land first so the USENET/forum posts can reference it as proof of seriousness.","files":null}]}